Using the SPARC training catalog (140 galaxies), an XGBoost model with physics-motivated features predicted outer dark-matter fraction under 5-fold cross-validation, reaching mean R² ≈ 0.77 and MAE ≈ 0.09. SHAP analysis found the strongest drivers were rotation-curve extent relative to disk size (R_max/R_disk), a dynamical-to-baryonic mass discrepancy proxy, and V_flat relative to baryonic mass, with gas fraction next; raw morphology and surface brightness mattered far less. In short, galaxies that are more extended kinematically and more mass-discrepant (often gas-richer systems) have higher dark-matter fractions, so these same observables can support estimates of relative dark content—and, with dynamical mass, rough dark-halo mass—without fitting full halo models galaxy by galaxy.